issue_context.body:
### Description

`RegexOptions.NonBacktracking` handles patterns like `(a|\b){2}` incorrectly, producing matches of the wrong length. The problem is related to loops with bodies that can accept the empty string, but only contextually due to use of anchors.

### Reproduction Steps

```
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
					
public class Program
{
	static void Compare(string input, string pattern)
	{
		Console.WriteLine($"Testing \"{input}\" with {pattern}");
		foreach (Match m in Regex.Matches(input, pattern))
		{
			Console.WriteLine($"Index {m.Index} length {m.Length} with None");
		}
		foreach (Match m in Regex.Matches(input, pattern, RegexOptions.NonBacktracking))
		{
			Console.WriteLine($"Index {m.Index} length {m.Length} with NonBacktracking");
		}
		Console.WriteLine();
	}
	
	public static void Main()
	{
		Compare("ac", @"(a|\b){2}");
	}
}
```

### Expected behavior

The program should print:
```
Testing "ac" with (a|\b){2}
Index 0 length 1 with None
Index 2 length 0 with None
Index 0 length 1 with NonBacktracking
Index 2 length 0 with NonBacktracking
```

### Actual behavior

The program prints:
```
Testing "ac" with (a|\b){2}
Index 0 length 1 with None
Index 2 length 0 with None
Index 0 length 0 with NonBacktracking
Index 2 length 0 with NonBacktracking
```
Notice that the first match with NonBacktracking is length 0.

### Regression?

This is not a regression, as RegexOptions.NonBacktracking is a new feature.

### Known Workarounds

_No response_

### Configuration

.NET 7 on https://dotnetfiddle.net/

### Other information

This problem is due to incorrect logic for handling alternation inside loop bodies. The bug was found by @veanes while proving the correctness of the derivation and pruning rules implemented in the new NonBacktracking feature.

